WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.661 --> 00:00:12.807
Parents have known for thousands of years that physical characteristics—such as curly hair or brown eyes—seem to run

2
00:00:12.847 --> 00:00:13.508
in the family.

3
00:00:14.028 --> 00:00:22.035
These characteristics are called inherited traits, and the transmission of traits from generation to generation is called heredity.

4
00:00:22.556 --> 00:00:29.029
Farmers realized the power of inherited traits very early on—thousands of years ago, when plants were first being

5
00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:32.111
cultivated and animals were first being domesticated.

6
00:00:32.771 --> 00:00:39.175
When selecting individuals to breed, a farmer would choose the stock with the best traits—the strongest oxen, the

7
00:00:39.215 --> 00:00:44.418
fastest horses, or the plants with the largest fruit—in the hope that their offspring would have the same

8
00:00:44.458 --> 00:00:51.842
characteristics. By the nineteenth century, it still wasn't known exactly how those traits were passed from one generation

9
00:00:51.882 --> 00:00:52.362
to another.

10
00:00:52.862 --> 00:00:57.837
Gregor Mendel, now known as the father of genetics, studied the hereditary patterns of pea plants.

11
00:00:58.578 --> 00:01:06.082
Starting in 1856, he spent years performing experiments and observing traits like flower color, seed shape, and stem

12
00:01:06.122 --> 00:01:13.086
length. For example, Mendel bred a pea plant that produced only green peas with another that produced only

13
00:01:13.146 --> 00:01:13.806
yellow peas.

14
00:01:14.487 --> 00:01:19.890
By doing this, he created a hybrid generation of pea plants, one that produced only yellow peas.

15
00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.792
Then he took some of those hybrid plants and bred them with each other.

16
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.144
The green trait reappeared in approximately a quarter of the peas in the next generation.

17
00:01:29.945 --> 00:01:35.790
This showed Mendel that individuals can pass a characteristic to the next generation even if they do not

18
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.791
express that trait.

19
00:01:37.371 --> 00:01:41.474
These observations led Mendel to conclude that every trait is controlled by "factors."

20
00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:47.179
Each individual receives two of these factors for each trait—one version from each parent.

21
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.764
Sometimes, one version is expressed, and at other times, the other version is expressed.

22
00:01:53.596 --> 00:01:58.938
Meanwhile, British scientist Charles Darwin had also been observing traits and experimenting.

23
00:01:59.538 --> 00:02:06.200
Darwin was puzzled by physical variations that had been absent in previous generations but suddenly appeared in a

24
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.900
new generation of an organism.

25
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:15.522
He proposed that the characteristics of a species change over time through a process he called "natural selection."

26
00:02:15.962 --> 00:02:21.224
Individuals that are not well adapted to their environment die young and do not have a chance to

27
00:02:21.284 --> 00:02:28.028
reproduce. Other individuals are better adapted, with features that make them more competitive in the struggle for survival

28
00:02:28.108 --> 00:02:28.548
and mates.

29
00:02:29.109 --> 00:02:31.230
These features are known as adaptations.

30
00:02:32.071 --> 00:02:37.975
Darwin observed examples of adaptation in a group of birds known now as Darwin's finches.

31
00:02:38.715 --> 00:02:44.839
Originally all one species, the finches had spread to different islands within the Galapagos Archipelago.

32
00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:51.524
Over time, the beaks of these finches changed, becoming suited to whatever food was available in each area.

33
00:02:52.188 --> 00:02:56.349
As a result, the one species had branched out into 14 species.

34
00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:04.732
Darwin's great insight—that over many generations, species can change dramatically to better fit their environments—is known as the

35
00:03:04.772 --> 00:03:05.792
theory of evolution.

36
00:03:06.372 --> 00:03:12.734
He published his ideas in 1859 in a groundbreaking book called On the Origin of Species.

37
00:03:13.314 --> 00:03:16.895
But Darwin still did not understand the biology behind this process.

38
00:03:17.755 --> 00:03:20.456
The modern science of heredity is known as genetics.

39
00:03:21.012 --> 00:03:26.576
In the decades following the work of Mendel and Darwin, scientists continued to study heredity.

40
00:03:27.136 --> 00:03:32.440
By the 1940s, scientists had deduced that at the center of the puzzle is a molecule that they

41
00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.442
called deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).

42
00:03:36.283 --> 00:03:40.646
DNA contains the biological set of instructions that make each species unique.

43
00:03:41.246 --> 00:03:42.847
What makes an elephant an elephant?

44
00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:45.689
DNA. What makes a tulip a tulip?

45
00:03:46.249 --> 00:03:54.395
DNA. Minor changes in DNA, called mutations, lead to changes in the structure and behavior of organisms and,

46
00:03:54.836 --> 00:03:58.539
over millions and millions of years, lead to new and different life forms.

47
00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:01.962
This is the process behind Darwin's theory of evolution.

48
00:04:02.762 --> 00:04:08.467
Although researchers were relatively sure that DNA was the key to inherited traits, they didn't know what it

49
00:04:08.507 --> 00:04:09.808
looked like.

50
00:04:09.908 --> 00:04:14.312
In 1953, the scientific community was racing to discover its structure.

51
00:04:15.072 --> 00:04:19.256
One team, at Cambridge University, was led by James Watson and Francis Crick.

52
00:04:19.796 --> 00:04:26.321
Researchers Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin, at King's College in London, were also working on the problem.

53
00:04:27.062 --> 00:04:33.087
Franklin used x-rays to make images of a DNA sample, and she suspected that the structure was a

54
00:04:33.147 --> 00:04:33.887
helix shape.

55
00:04:34.728 --> 00:04:37.831
Her partner showed Franklin's research to Watson and Crick.

56
00:04:38.431 --> 00:04:40.573
The Cambridge team solved the rest of the puzzle.

57
00:04:41.013 --> 00:04:46.798
DNA was helix-shaped, but it was a double helix—a double-stranded, twisted molecule.

58
00:04:47.298 --> 00:04:53.857
This discovery won Watson, Crick, and Wilkins the 1962 Nobel Prize for Physiology or Medicine.

59
00:04:53.877 --> 00:04:56.898
(Franklin had died of illness in 1958.)

60
00:04:57.618 --> 00:05:02.420
The work of these two teams allowed other scientists to unravel the mystery of DNA.

61
00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.581
DNA is found in the nucleus of every cell.

62
00:05:06.161 --> 00:05:08.801
It is organized into structures called chromosomes.

63
00:05:09.362 --> 00:05:11.022
Chromosomes are made up of genes.

64
00:05:11.582 --> 00:05:18.616
Human chromosomes have tens of thousands of genes, and all of these genes together—known as a genome—control an

65
00:05:18.716 --> 00:05:20.037
organism's characteristics.

66
00:05:20.557 --> 00:05:25.218
Further discoveries in the twentieth century paved the way for amazing advances in genetics.

67
00:05:25.798 --> 00:05:29.039
In the 1970s, "genetic engineering" became possible.

68
00:05:29.779 --> 00:05:34.861
Scientists learned how to take a gene from one organism and combine it with the DNA of another.

69
00:05:35.421 --> 00:05:41.022
For example, a gene that keeps flounder from freezing in cold water was cut from the chromosome of

70
00:05:41.062 --> 00:05:43.783
the fish and inserted into a tomato's DNA.

71
00:05:44.363 --> 00:05:47.404
This change resulted in a tomato less vulnerable to frost.

72
00:05:48.032 --> 00:05:53.156
Genes have been inserted into many other crops to make the plants resistant to disease or to increase

73
00:05:53.216 --> 00:06:00.462
production. Although genetically modified crops are becoming quite common, many believe that they pose a significant danger to

74
00:06:00.482 --> 00:06:04.726
the environment and human health because their long-term effects are not known.

75
00:06:05.647 --> 00:06:10.431
Promising—and somewhat less controversial—advances have also been made in the area of human genetics.

76
00:06:11.031 --> 00:06:16.776
In the 1980s, scientists developed methods to compare the DNA sequence of different individuals.

77
00:06:17.344 --> 00:06:22.327
DNA left behind at the scene of a crime has been used as evidence in court, both to

78
00:06:22.367 --> 00:06:26.189
prosecute criminals and to set free people who have been wrongly accused.

79
00:06:26.709 --> 00:06:29.991
DNA sequencing techniques are also useful in the field of medicine.

80
00:06:30.671 --> 00:06:37.395
Studying families with rare genetic disorders has allowed doctors to trace the genetic basis of disease through generations.

81
00:06:38.055 --> 00:06:43.298
This kind of genetic tracking helps doctors to predict the likelihood of a person getting a disease and

82
00:06:43.338 --> 00:06:46.280
to diagnose it—although not to cure the illness.

83
00:06:46.988 --> 00:06:53.791
However, some genetic diseases can now be treated by replacing damaged genes with healthy ones, a practice called

84
00:06:53.831 --> 00:06:54.571
gene therapy.

85
00:06:55.532 --> 00:07:02.255
In many cases, it's not possible or ethical to experiment on humans, so scientists often rely on other

86
00:07:02.355 --> 00:07:08.457
organisms, ones that reproduce rapidly such as mice or fruit flies, to learn more about the effects of

87
00:07:08.497 --> 00:07:09.178
gene therapy.

88
00:07:09.798 --> 00:07:14.100
The twenty-first century continues to be a time of technological advancement in genetics.

89
00:07:14.752 --> 00:07:20.277
The first milestone of the twenty-first century was the completion of the Human Genome Project in 2003.

90
00:07:20.877 --> 00:07:26.122
Researchers now know the DNA sequence of all human genes, and are making a detailed map of each

91
00:07:26.162 --> 00:07:32.527
chromosome. Doctors are able to take a sample of a person's DNA, scan it, and determine his or

92
00:07:32.547 --> 00:07:33.448
her genetic risk.

93
00:07:34.069 --> 00:07:38.993
New mysteries and concerns will undoubtedly arise as scientists continue to explore genetics.

94
00:07:39.974 --> 00:07:45.799
But their discoveries will also answer many questions about the biology of the world's species and the code

95
00:07:45.819 --> 00:07:46.199
of life.
